# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows checks text files out with CRLF by default. That is harmless
# for anything this repository compiles or parses, and NOT harmless for a file
# compared byte for byte.
#
# internal/engine/testdata/selector_golden.txt is exactly that: 3200 rows
# freezing every reachable answer of chooseSource, asserted against the running
# code as whole bytes. Checked out with CRLF it fails on windows-latest and
# nowhere else, reporting "3200 of 3200 rows changed" -- every row differing
# only by an invisible \r. Worse, the diff it prints to justify that claim
# arrives BLANK, because the carriage return sends the cursor back over the line
# just written. The failure therefore accuses the selector of a total rewrite
# and shows nothing to support it, which is the most expensive shape a CI
# failure can take.
#
# Pinned by directory rather than by name so the next golden file inherits the
# fix instead of rediscovering the bug.
**/testdata/** text eol=lf

# ui/src/lib/contract/ is the same problem one directory over, and it found
# this file the hard way: the schedule-action contract is generated by a Go
# test and compared byte for byte against the committed copy, so a CRLF
# checkout failed on windows-latest and nowhere else -- printing a "got" and a
# "want" that were, to the eye, character for character identical. A diff that
# looks like it proves the test wrong is worse than no diff.
#
# By directory again, for the reason given above: the next contract file
# inherits the fix rather than rediscovering it. These are also read by vitest
# on the UI side, which does not care either way.
ui/src/lib/contract/** text eol=lf

# The acceptance suites and their shared libraries run under bash on Linux and
# macOS runners. A CRLF checkout makes the shebang line end in \r, and the
# kernel then looks for an interpreter literally named "bash\r" -- reported as
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory", which reads as a missing shell
# rather than a line-ending problem. Nothing runs these on Windows today; this
# is here so that stays true by construction rather than by luck.
*.sh text eol=lf
